+To distinguish the information rewarded by the score from that used in the allocation, define $m_t=p_{t,2}$ and $a_t=p_{t,3}/(p_{t,1}+p_{t,3})$. Let $z_{t+1}=\mathbf{1}\{y_{t+1}=2\}$ and $u_{t+1}=\mathbf{1}\{y_{t+1}=3\}$. Positivity from smoothing makes these quantities well defined. The log loss factors exactly as
+\begin{equation}
+\begin{split}
+\ell_{t+1}={}&-z_{t+1}\log m_t-(1-z_{t+1})\log(1-m_t)\\
+&-(1-z_{t+1})\{u_{t+1}\log a_t+(1-u_{t+1})\log(1-a_t)\}.
+\end{split}
+\label{eq:scorefactor}
+\end{equation}
+The first line evaluates middle versus outer terciles; the second evaluates upper versus lower conditional on an outer-tercile outcome. Meanwhile,
+\begin{equation}
+p_{t,3}-p_{t,1}=(1-m_t)(2a_t-1).
+\label{eq:tiltfactor}
+\end{equation}
+Improving the middle-versus-outer forecast need not improve upper-versus-lower discrimination. In particular, if $a_t=1/2$, changing $m_t$ does not change the allocation. These identities identify possible channels, not their empirical contributions: the aggregate results do not establish which component generated the score gains. Outer-tercile membership is relative to historical cutpoints, so neither component is itself a test of return sign or a direct measure of volatility.
