+\subsection{Forecast accuracy}
+Table~\ref{tab:scores} reports the lowest log loss and Brier score for the unrestricted chain on all assets. Reversible-minus-unrestricted log-loss differences are 0.001315, 0.000685 and 0.001595 for SPY, QQQ and IWM. All primary intervals contain zero (Table~\ref{tab:intervals}). Unrestricted-minus-IID intervals lie below zero, conditionally and pointwise. Related ETFs are not independent replications. Online Appendix A supplies development/validation context and full inference sensitivities. Relative to IID mean log loss, the unrestricted reductions are approximately 0.484\%, 1.065\% and 0.450\% (Table~\ref{tab:scale}). These are reductions in a scoring loss, not percentage-point gains in classification accuracy or investment returns. The intervals for reversible-minus-unrestricted differences neither demonstrate a reversible advantage nor exclude every practically relevant difference.
 
+\subsection{Absolute performance and incremental economic value}
 All nine allocations lose capital at primary costs (Table~\ref{tab:economics}; Figure~\ref{fig:wealth}). SPY unrestricted cumulative return falls from 31.21\% gross to $-0.47\%$ at 1 bps and $-67.05\%$ at 5 bps per side. QQQ yields 41.66\% gross and 7.40\% at 1 bps, but a net loss of 64.52\% at 5 bps per side; IWM already loses 18.03\% gross (Table~\ref{tab:costs}). These are full-period cumulative returns, not annualized rates.
 
-The mechanism runs through the upper-minus-lower probability difference: $w_t-0.5=0.5(p_{t,3}-p_{t,1})$. Across saved final forecasts, all allocations lie between 0.4708 and 0.5273; this is a descriptive audit of frozen predictions, not a new fitted specification. Consequently, daily liquidation and re-entry produce $T_{t+1}=2w_t\approx1$, charging approximately 5 bps of portfolio capital per day at the primary cost. Figure~\ref{fig:calibration} shows bin-average probabilities, not the range of individual forecasts, and does not identify low signal-to-noise as a cause. Small upper/lower differences compress exposure around one-half; the rule remains invested even without a probability tilt. State scores also omit within-state return magnitudes, so no score-based break-even threshold follows. At only 1 bps per side, unrestricted SPY and IWM still return $-0.47\%$ and $-37.83\%$, whereas QQQ returns 7.40\% (Table~\ref{tab:costs}); profitability is cost- and asset-dependent.
+Across saved final forecasts, all allocations lie between 0.4708 and 0.5273 (Online Appendix Table A5). This is a descriptive audit of frozen predictions. The rule therefore implements a small probability-driven adjustment around a persistent half exposure. Daily liquidation and re-entry produce $T_{t+1}\approx1$, charging approximately 5 bps of portfolio capital per day at the primary cost.
+
+The scale of this drag can be seen without a market-data model. If exposure were exactly one-half and every underlying intraday return were zero, wealth after 2,766 sessions at $c=0.0005$ would be $(1-c)^{2766}\approx0.250738$, a loss of 74.93\%. This is a counterfactual accounting illustration, not an attribution of 74.93 percentage points of the observed losses. Actual wealth also reflects return realizations, changing exposure and compounding. It explains why absolute losses under the fixed rule must be separated from the incremental value of its probability adjustment.
+
+Table~\ref{tab:scale} applies Equation~\eqref{eq:paireddecomp} to unrestricted-minus-IID mean net differences and retained mean weights. The approximate daily net gains are 0.0425 bps for SPY, 0.0200 bps for QQQ and 0.0728 bps for IWM. Their implied gross gains are 0.0381, 0.0249 and 0.0703 bps; mean cost changes are $-0.0045$, 0.0049 and $-0.0025$ bps. Simple annualization of the mean net differences gives 0.1071, 0.0504 and 0.1834 percentage points per 252 sessions. These small relative improvements coexist with negative absolute performance. They are arithmetic rescalings of retained point estimates, not new hypothesis tests or compound annual returns.
+
+Figure~\ref{fig:calibration} shows bin-average probabilities and does not establish which score component in Equation~\eqref{eq:scorefactor} generated the gains. The allocation ignores within-state return magnitudes, so aggregate state scores alone cannot determine an economic break-even cost. At 1 bps per side, unrestricted SPY and IWM return $-0.47\%$ and $-37.83\%$, whereas QQQ returns 7.40\% (Table~\ref{tab:costs}); profitability remains cost- and asset-dependent. No alternative allocation is selected from these outcomes.
 
 IID exposure is nearly constant, ranging from 0.499864 to 0.500000 across the three assets. For an exactly constant $w>0$, $R_{t+1}=w(r_{t+1}-2c)$ implies $\operatorname{Sharpe}(wX)=\operatorname{Sharpe}(X)$, since the mean and standard deviation scale together. This explains IID and fully invested intraday Sharpe ratios agreeing to three decimals in Table~\ref{tab:economics}; the observed near-constant weights do not imply exact equality beyond rounding, equal drawdowns or equal cumulative returns.
 
 QQQ provides a particularly clear contrast. Its unrestricted-minus-IID log-loss gain is largest ($-0.011697$, versus $-0.005314$ for SPY and $-0.004942$ for IWM), yet its primary mean-net-return and Sharpe-difference intervals contain zero; the latter is $[-0.008712,0.017061]$ (Online Appendix Table A3). SPY and IWM have positive primary relative-Sharpe intervals (Tables A2 and A4), despite smaller score gains. These conditional, pointwise comparisons illustrate that score improvements need not rank economic improvements monotonically; they are not a formal test of cross-asset differences, and positive relative Sharpe does not imply positive absolute profitability.
 
